    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟠📝 **Fix · docs: SettingsScopeBridge was inserted between WorkspacesBridge and its JSDoc**\n\nThe new interface sits right after the `/** Workspaces (ADR 0032). Item-scoped calls … stale-workspace … */` block (lines 167-171). That block now comes directly before `SettingsScopeBridge`'s own doc comment, so `WorkspacesBridge` (line 197) has lost its documentation, and the workspaces text is left dangling above the wrong interface. Move `SettingsScopeBridge` and its comment above line 167, or below `WorkspacesBridge`.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 75698b79: `SettingsScopeBridge` and its doc now sit above `WorkspacesBridge`'s JSDoc, which is back on its interface."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438866305,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/workspace-settings.ts",
    "line": 241,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: A failed machine-record write after the folder write makes this Mac's own overrides go pending**\n\n`applyFolder` writes the folder file first (line 241), puts the new read in the cache (line 242), and only then records the new sha256 with `decision: \"use\"` (lines 243-246). If `machine.update` throws (for example a disk error in `atomicWriteFile`), `apply` returns `failed`. By then the folder file on disk already has the new bytes, but the machine record still holds the old hash. `folderState` (settings-overrides.ts:168) then reports the file as `pending`, so `overridesOf` drops every folder override for this workspace, including the ones that were active before the edit. The user is then asked to confirm values this Mac wrote itself. Possible fixes: if the record write fails, put the previous file back, or retry recording the hash. At minimum, keep the cache consistent with what was recorded.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Real, fixed in 75698b79. `writeFolderOverrides` now takes a `beforeWrite` hook and the hash is recorded BEFORE the file is replaced; if recording throws, the file is untouched, and if the write throws the previous record is put back. Test: 'a failed machine record writes nothing to the folder and leaves the active overrides alone'."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438866310,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/workspace-settings.ts",
    "line": 200,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: A pending folder file blocks clearing `uploadMode` in the same call, and copyToAll may not report it**\n\nIn `apply`, when `applyFolder` returns `pending` or `unreadable`, the function returns at line 200 before it reaches the machine-bound `uploadMode` branch (lines 202-209). So `resetOverrides(id, [\"screenshotCopy\", \"uploadMode\"])` on a workspace whose folder file is pending leaves `uploadMode` overridden. `copyToAll` (lines 324-328) then adds the workspace to `keptOwn` only when the pending file holds one of the wanted keys. A workspace whose `uploadMode` override survived but whose pending file holds none of the wanted keys is therefore missing from `keptOwn`, and \"all\" quietly means \"some\". Today's UI never sends `uploadMode` together with folder keys: the SectionBanner keys in settings-pages.tsx are `[recordingQuality, exportQuality]`, `[showBrandBadge]` and `[screenshotCopy]`. But the IPC handler (index.ts:545-550) accepts any mix. Two possible fixes: apply the machine-bound key whatever the folder outcome, or check `uploadMode` in the `pending` branch of `copyToAll`.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Real, fixed in 75698b79. The machine-bound `uploadMode` is applied first and no longer depends on the folder outcome, so a pending or unreadable file cannot keep it from being cleared. A workspace that cannot be cleared is named in `keptOwn` only if it can still hold one of the keys. Test: 'clears uploadMode even when the folder file is pending'."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438866311,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/renderer/src/pages/settings/scope/settings-scope.tsx",
    "line": 236,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡⚡ **Consider · perf: Every scope consumer runs its own useAppSettings fetch and listener, even inside the provider**\n\n`useSettingsScope` always calls `useScopeState(shared === null)`. That is how hooks have to be called, but `useScopeState` calls `useAppSettings()` (line 64) on every call, and that hook's effect isn't gated: each call runs `window.electronAPI.getSettings()` and registers an `onSettingsChanged` listener (`use-app-settings.ts:15-18`).\n\nInside `SettingsScopeProvider`, every `ScopeTag`, `ScopeSelector`, `ScopeDot` (×3), `ScopeLegend`, `ScopeLine`, `AppWideBadge`, `SectionBanner`, `ScopeNotice` and `useScopedSetting` therefore makes an extra IPC round trip and adds an extra listener. Every settings change then re-renders each of these components through its own unused state.\n\nPossible fix: split the provider-less fallback into a separate component, or move the `useAppSettings` call so it only runs when `enabled`. For example, pass `update` in, or call the IPC `updateSettings` directly inside `setValue`.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Real, fixed in 75698b79. `useScopeState` no longer calls `useAppSettings`: the All-level write goes straight through `updateSettings` and every window follows the `settings:changed` it causes. A provider now costs one scoped read plus one listener, not one per component. (`useScopedSetting` keeps its single `useAppSettings`, one per row, as the rows had before.)"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438866317,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/renderer/src/features/recording/hooks/use-recording-setup.test.tsx",
    "line": 294,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟠🧪 **Fix · tests: Test asserts on an object that was already built, so it can never fail**\n\n`take` is the plain object that `resolveInput()` returned at line 292. The broadcast at line 293 cannot change a value that already exists, so `expect(take.frameRate).toBe(24)` passes no matter what the hook does. If the hook rebuilt the input from the latest settings, this test would still pass. To test what the name says, check the input the recorder actually uses after the broadcast. If the recorder calls `resolveInput` lazily, call it again after `broadcast(...)` and assert that the result still has `frameRate: 24`. Otherwise, make the assertion against whatever value the hook keeps for the take that is running.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fair: the assertion could not fail. Replaced in 75698b79 with 'the quality is read when the start is requested, not when the hook mounted': it resolves the start input, broadcasts a switch, resolves again, and expects 24 then 60 fps. What I can honestly assert is that the value is read at request time; the recorder keeps the object it was handed."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438866320,
    "path": "packages/i18n/messages/en.json",
    "line": 2689,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: copiedKept always uses the plural verb**\n\n`copiedKept` reads `\"{names} keep their own value…\"`. When only one workspace can't be cleared, the toast says something like \"Work keep their own value\", which is ungrammatical. The Spanish string at es.json:2689 has the same problem (\"{names} conservan\"). To fix it, pass a `count` and use an ICU plural, as `bannerDiffers` already does: `{count, plural, one {{names} keeps its own value…} other {{names} keep their own value…}}`.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 75698b79 with an ICU plural on `count` in both `en.json` and `es.json` ('Bravo keeps its own value because it couldn't be changed' / 'conservan' → 'conserva')."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439021325,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/workspace-settings.ts",
    "line": 123,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: A refresh that starts in the middle of a write can still put the old sha256 back**\n\nThe generation check only drops a probe that started before a `remember` and finished after the next one. `applyFolder` itself calls `remember` twice: once for the pre-write read (line 287) and once after the write (line 324). Between those two calls it records the new hash in the machine file (the `beforeWrite` hook, lines 308-312) and only then replaces the file.\n\nHere is the failing order. A `refresh` (for example the capture-start one at `index.ts:147`) starts after line 287, so it captures that generation. Its probe reads the old bytes before the rename and returns before line 324. The generation has not changed, so line 124 caches the old read with the old sha256. The machine record already holds the new hash, so `folderState` reports `pending`. `overridesOf` then drops every folder override, and `changed()` fires. A capture that snapshots right after that refresh records without this Mac's overrides, which is the failure the new comment at lines 66-70 describes. The window is narrower than before but still there.\n\nOne option: treat an in-flight write as a reason to drop the probe too. For example, also return when `this.queues.has(id)` after the probe. The write's own read and `remember` are fresher in that case anyway. The other option is to bump the generation inside the `beforeWrite` hook and again after the write.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Real, fixed in d9accc88. The generation counter moved to where it belongs: `applyFolder` marks a write in flight (and bumps a write epoch before and after it), a refresh whose read overlapped a write is dropped, and while a write runs the overrides answer is held at what it was before it began, so recording the new hash first cannot switch this Mac's own overrides off for a moment. Test: a capture-start refresh in the middle of a write leaves `{ screenshotCopy: manual }` active (it fails without the hold)."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439021328,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/workspace-settings.ts",
    "line": 122,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: Between two refreshes, the first to finish now wins, not the freshest read**\n\n`refresh` also bumps the generation through `remember`. So when two refreshes of the same workspace overlap, whichever finishes first is cached, and the other is dropped along with its `changed()` notification. The comment says the landed read \"is the newer one\", but that is not true for two refreshes. Suppose refresh A starts first and reads quickly, a sync then rewrites the file, and refresh B (started later) reads the new bytes. If A finishes first, B's newer read is discarded, and the cache stays stale until the next refresh. Before this change, the last refresh to finish won.\n\nRefreshes can overlap. `getScoped(\"all\")` refreshes every workspace (line 179), and `getScoped(scope)` and `copyToAll` refresh too (lines 208, 410), while the capture start (`index.ts:147`) and activation (`index.ts:410`, `466`) refresh on their own.\n\nOne fix is to bump a separate counter only from the write paths (`applyFolder`, `resolvePendingNow`) and compare against that. Another is to give each probe a start sequence number and drop a read only if a later-started read has already landed.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Real, fixed in d9accc88. `refresh` no longer bumps anything shared: each refresh takes a ticket when it starts and is applied only if no refresh that started later has already been applied, so the last to start wins whichever finishes first. Test: the slow first refresh holds an older read, the later one finishes first, and the cache keeps the newer one (it fails without the ticket check)."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439105820,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/workspace-settings.ts",
    "line": 155,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: Disk reads in resolvePending and applyFolder no longer fence off a slower refresh**\n\nBefore this change, every `remember` bumped the generation. That included the fresh disk reads in `resolvePendingNow` (line 395) and the read at the start of `applyFolder` (line 319), which is the only read when it returns `pending`, `unreadable` or `unchanged`. Now only `beginWrite`/`endWrite` move `writeEpochs`, and they run only when the folder file is actually rewritten.\n\nHere is the order that fails. A refresh starts and reads the old bytes (sha A). A sync then replaces the file with sha B. The user clicks \"Use them\", so `resolvePendingNow` reads B and records `{sha256: B, decision: \"use\"}`. Then the slow refresh finishes. `writing` is 0, the epoch has not changed and its ticket is still the newest, so line 150 caches read A. Record B does not match read A, so the state is `pending` and the overrides the user just accepted are dropped until the next refresh.\n\nThe window is narrow because it needs a sync in the middle of the race, but it is the same kind of race the new comment at lines 66-71 says is closed. One possible fix: bump `writeEpochs` (or the refresh ticket/applied counter) in `resolvePendingNow` and at the top of `applyFolder`'s read, so these fresh reads count as newer than any refresh already in flight.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Real, fixed in 543053c4. The fresh read an edit starts from and the one an answer takes now bump the write epoch (`fence`), so a refresh already in flight drops what it read. Test: a slow refresh holding an older read cannot undo \"Use them\" for the changed file (it fails without the fence)."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439105831,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/workspace-settings.ts",
    "line": 447,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: copyToAll refuses `uploadMode` because of an unrelated folder file**\n\nThe new guard returns `unreadable` whenever the folder file cannot be read, whatever keys are wanted. `uploadMode` is machine-bound. `computeOverrides` (line 196) reads it from `deps.machine` whether or not the folder file can be read, so `copyToAll(id, [\"uploadMode\"])` could still report what the workspace shows. Today's UI does not seem to send `uploadMode` to copyToAll, but the IPC accepts any overridable key. If that is meant to work, limit the guard to calls whose `wanted` includes a folder key.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Real, fixed in 543053c4. The guard applies only when a folder key is among the copied ones; `uploadMode` is machine-bound and copies past an unreadable folder file. Test added."
      }
    ]
  }
]

